ce-book-text-pantheon-gods-sileita = The Pantheon of Sileita is divided into three great ranks: [bold]Absolutes, Elder Gods, and Lesser Gods[/bold]. Faith in the gods influences the political, spiritual, and magical life of all peoples of Sileita. Some deities are forbidden from worship, others are venerated as official patrons of countries or organizations.
    {"[italic]'I am the Observer, a templar of the Order of Keepers of Truths, tasked with gathering knowledge of all gods of Sileita without bias. We do not pray. We record. No god is worthy of worship until understood in full. None is above another. All are parts of a complex picture.'[/italic]"}

    {"[head=2]The Absolutes — The First Powers[/head]"}

    {"[head=3][color=purple]Tomer[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Fickle, The First Contradiction, The Shaper. The Forsaken.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Chaos, metamorphosis, biological and metaphysical evolution.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: A spiral of spreading forms.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: His physical form is imprisoned in a divine jail. His 'comings' are catastrophes: mutations, eruptions of alien biomes, flesh falling from the sky. Tomer's influence — glimpses of his energy capable of altering the world around.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: He cannot be called evil — he is beyond concepts of good. It is blind curiosity in destruction. He changes for the sake of change itself. The mind of a pulsing world that dreams of becoming something new.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: He is interested in them as material. He is not an executioner, but a craftsman. Dispassionate, yet attentive.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: Dangerous not so much for his power, but because he infects minds with the idea of destruction as a method of progress. His ban by law is justified. His teaching is sweet poison.

    {"[head=3][color=purple]Altaran[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Flickering Court, The Flow's Course, The Watcher Through Ages.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Time, temporal currents, judgment, order.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: An eternal circle with inner clocks running in different directions.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Never appears in flesh. Has never been seen in Sileita, though a presence has been noted in the Void.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Absolutely impartial. There is no anger in him, only equation. He is repulsive to mortals because he does not forgive — but neither does he take revenge. He is the unofficial leader of the entire pantheon and the governing authority.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Sees them as units in a model. He does not deny the significance of the individual, but only acknowledges their influence on the flow of time.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: If all the gods vanished, Altaran would remain to watch their work crumble.

    {"[head=3][color=purple]Relafir and Vestris[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Twins of Passage, The Key and The Lock, The Primordial Gates. The Wolf and the Sheep.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Life, death, soul, reincarnation, the Void.
    • [bold]Symbol of faith[/bold]: Intertwined circles with an eye at the center.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: At the moment of birth — Vestris watches. At the moment of death — Relafir escorts. In deep silence and in the last breath — both. Only Relafir has physically appeared, on the judgment day of Bafamir.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]:
    - Vestris — soft and pensive, like a spring morning.
    - Relafir — reserved and inevitable, like the night.
     They do not argue. [bold]Their unity is fundamental.[/bold]
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: They do not judge, encourage, or persuade. They simply are. They accompany without interfering.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: They are fearsome not for their power, but because they have the right to use it. Necromancers and skilled healers may adopt faith in the twins to study resurrection and rebirth.

    {"[head=2]The Elder Gods — The Firstborn[/head]"}

    {"[head=3][color=forestgreen]Irumel[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Fallen Shield, The Faithful, The Last of the Starry.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Protection, honor, self-sacrifice.
    • [bold]Symbol of faith[/bold]: A shattered shield emitting light.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: [bold]Believed to be dead[/bold] in the divine war. But in the ruins of temples, his presence is felt as a call to duty. They say a wounded silhouette can be seen in former places of his faith.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: He was the embodiment of service. They say he would stay silent for hours, yet with a single glance convince an army.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Saw in them, perhaps, more than they deserved. Believed they were worth protecting at any cost.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: If he is indeed dead, then gods are mortal. That is troubling.

    {"[head=3][color=forestgreen]Devias / Fenor[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Silver Shadow, The Whisper in Sleep, The Listener of the Unnamed.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Dreams, secrets, foresight, concealment.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: An eye vertically covered by a finger.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Almost never appears physically, but through rituals one can speak with him briefly — though the price is great.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Quiet and benevolent, yet unsettling. Speaks in hints. Possesses monstrous knowledge.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Warns, but does not interfere. Whoever listens — is saved. Whoever demands — goes mad.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: Not an enemy. But not an ally either.

    {"[head=3][color=forestgreen]Jelarion[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Black Flame, The Bloody Teacher, The Echo of Resentment.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Vengeance, fury, retribution, wrath.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: A flaming heart pierced by a dagger.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Comes at the moment of a vow of vengeance. Often not directly, but through visions or hallucinations at the moment of the oath.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Blazingly bright, with predatory charisma.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Loves their pain, but not for suffering's sake.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: He is dangerous not for granting power, but for making the victim sweet.

    {"[head=3][color=forestgreen]Mercas[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Embalming Light, The Merciful Brother, He Who Cleanses.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Healing, purification, resurrection.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: A white palm driving away darkness.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Appears in miracles: when the fatal disappears without a trace, when a curse shatters.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Calm. Does not judge, but does not forget. Speaks softly, acts precisely.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Sees in them the potential for restoration.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: Of them all, he is the closest to what is called 'good'. But even he is not without shadow. Purification can become obsession.

    {"[head=2]The Lesser Gods — Ascended or Second Descendants[/head]"}

    {"[head=3][color=blue]Rikhyard / Helmir[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Iron Gaze, The Patron of Duels, The Eternal Warlord.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: War, battle, honor.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: Crossed swords above a helmet.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Often in person, in the body of a soldier of any race, hiding his true essence. Speaks through the blazing inspiration of a warrior.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Crude but honest. His respect must be earned. He is not about victory — he is about the fight.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Loves those who fight not for killing, but for dignity. Tieflings are the native beings of his personal war plane.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: Dangerous where the cult turns war into a game. But necessary where honor stands above fear.

    {"[head=3][color=blue]Arkhofeya[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Mother of Embrace, The Warming Wound, The Flower After the Storm.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Love, kindness, family.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: An open heart with petals.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Her image may appear during wedding ceremonies and other powerfully emotional moments.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Sincere and compassionate. Speaks like a mother.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Accepts everyone. Even the fallen. Especially them.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: Her faith is often mocked as 'weak'. Yet it is she who holds people back from the final brink.

    {"[head=3][color=blue]Lumera[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Guide in the Night, The Quiet Teacher, The Eye of the Moon.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Night, stars, knowledge, teaching.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: A crescent moon from which rays stream forth.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Often physically, in the guise of a harpy. She is one of the few who truly walks among people. She often appears in Kraikhel. Known for her public lectures, though her face is almost never remembered.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Wise, ironic, caring. Teaches not with words, but with questions.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Values those who strive for knowledge and those who have risen from darkness.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: Liars fear her. Priests try to find a connection between Lumera and the crimson moon.

    {"[head=3][color=blue]Silforia[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Wind of Thirst, The Mistress of Adventures, The Fearless.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Adventure, exploration, freedom.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: Leaves in the wind.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Often travels incognito. She could be anyone: a beggar, an old man, a guildmaster.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Energetic, wild, with infectious laughter. Loves a challenge.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Values those who take risks. Despises those who live locked behind fear.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: The only god you'd want to sit around a campfire with. She laughs both at you and with you. Former greater spirit of wind who received the blessing of Relafir and Vestris and became a goddess after repelling a leviathan attack.

    {"[head=3][color=blue]Aleona[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Judgment of Light, The Steel Guardian, The Eye of the Empire.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Justice, protection, law.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: Scales surrounded by radiance.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: In the halls of justice of the Zellas Empire. She may appear in person if a trial threatens to become a farce.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Impeccably cold-blooded. She does not turn away from pain, but does not accept tears as an argument.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: Sees them as subjects of justice. Believes in redemption, but punishes without hesitation.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: The Empire calls her their goddess. But she serves not the throne — but the truth. And that makes her more fearsome than the entire pantheon.

    {"[head=3][color=blue]Osif[/color][/head]"}

    • [bold]Names[/bold]: [italic]The Storm, Insatiability, The Ocean Depths.[/italic]
    • [bold]Idea[/bold]: Waywardness, defiance, sea madness.
    • [bold]Symbol[/bold]: Two waves moving toward each other.
    • [bold]Presence[/bold]: Almost never appears in the world, but his child — the Leviathan — dwells in the depths of oceans and seas.
    • [bold]Nature[/bold]: Explosive temperament, you never know what to expect from him.
    • [bold]Attitude toward mortals[/bold]: May both aid and sink entire coastlines and fleets. His child despises every living being.
    • [bold]Commentary[/bold]: The Deity of Oceans, Waters and Storms. During the divine war, he supported Tomer and, with the energy of Lumera, spawned the Leviathan — a monster that, during the war with Yantakiniki, sank nearly all ships of the Zellassian Empire and fought in a forgotten era with Silforia.
